From: Ubuntu gts9uwifi port contributors <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5: mask handover IRQ after first signal

The handover interrupt is a one-shot notification for each remoteproc start.
Samsung's SM8550 ADSP keeps pulsing the SMP2P handover edge after sensor clients
attach.  The first edge performs the handover correctly; every later edge only
logs "Handover signaled, but it already happened" and can consume most of one
CPU in the threaded IRQ handler.

Mask the IRQ after completing the first handover.  Avoid adding a second
disable depth in unprepare(), so prepare() continues to rearm it exactly once
for the next remoteproc start.

diff --git a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
index be9ac697893a..97787ac6b54f 100644
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5.c
@@ -78,7 +78,8 @@ int qcom_q6v5_prepare(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5)
  */
 int qcom_q6v5_unprepare(struct qcom_q6v5 *q6v5)
 {
-	disable_irq(q6v5->handover_irq);
+	if (!q6v5->handover_issued)
+		disable_irq(q6v5->handover_irq);
 	q6v5_load_state_toggle(q6v5, false);
 
 	/* Disable interconnect vote, in case handover never happened */
@@ -174,6 +175,7 @@ static irqreturn_t q6v5_handover_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 	icc_set_bw(q6v5->path, 0, 0);
 
 	q6v5->handover_issued = true;
+	disable_irq_nosync(irq);
 
 	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
